2. Pre-flight
-------------
  [WARN] swap: 2.05 GiB of swap in use.
         Small, but re-check after loading the model.
  [WARN] dimm-population: EDAC reports 256 GiB installed but the kernel sees 216 GiB -- a 40 GiB gap.
         That gap is about the size of one or more DIMMs. If a memory channel is
         unpopulated you lose bandwidth in proportion, and no software setting
         recovers it. Confirm with `sudo dmidecode -t memory` and compare the
         number of populated slots against your CPU's channel count.
  nothing blocking.

3. Search space
---------------
  The cost model is used here and nowhere else: to pick which configurations are
  worth launching. Everything reported below this point is measured.

  model is dense, 28 layers, 0.85 GB read per token
  no draft model given -- `wirl find-draft <model>` searches for a compatible one
  predicted starting point: --n-gpu-layers 28 (bracketing 27..28 empirically)

4. VRAM boundary (measured)
---------------------------
  Bisecting by actually launching the server. A predicted boundary is a guess
  about allocator behaviour; the real one is wherever it stops starting.

  bracketing the VRAM boundary between --n-gpu-layers 27 and 28
    trying --n-gpu-layers 27 ... starts (2779 MiB)
    trying --n-gpu-layers 28 ... starts (2837 MiB)
  largest --n-gpu-layers that starts: 28

